# Sum of Sortable Integers

## Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isRotation(vector<int>& vec1, vector<int>& vec2) {
        vector<int> temp = vec1;
        sort(temp.begin(), temp.end());

        if (temp != vec2) {
            return false;
        }

        int n = vec1.size();
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (vec1[i] > vec1[(i + 1) % n]) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count <= 1;
    }

    int sortableIntegers(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> sorted = nums;
        sort(sorted.begin(), sorted.end());

        int result = 0;

        for (int k = 1; k <= n; k++) {
            if (n % k != 0) continue;

            bool valid = true;

            for (int start = 0; start < n; start += k) {
                vector<int> vec1(
                    nums.begin() + start,
                    nums.begin() + start + k
                );

                vector<int> vec2(
                    sorted.begin() + start,
                    sorted.begin() + start + k
                );

                if (!isRotation(vec1, vec2)) {
                    valid = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (valid) {
                result += k;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

## Approach

1. Create a sorted copy of the original array.
2. Iterate through all divisors `k` of `n`.
3. Divide the original array and sorted array into consecutive blocks of length `k`.
4. Use `isRotation()` to check whether each original block can be sorted by cyclic rotation and matches its corresponding sorted block.
5. If all blocks pass, add `k` to the result.

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n^2 \log n)\) upper bound.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(n)\).

## Key Observation

A block can be sorted by cyclic rotation if it has at most one strict descent, including the circular comparison between its last and first elements.
